      "q": "3. The phrases \u2018sounding sight\u2019 and \u2018hopped home\u2019 are examples of ___________. (alliteration/simile)",
      "a": "wer:\nThe poem uses vivid imagery to present a humorous and nonsensical scene that is amusing yet thought-provoking. The poem is structured in a dialogue form.\nThe phrases \u201csounding sight\u201d and \u201chopped home\u201d are examples of alliteration.\nV. Rewrite the following line from the poem in the correct order.\nQuestion 1. But never had I seen before Such a funny sounding sight.\nAnswer:\nBut I had never seen a sight that sounded so funny before.\nQuestion 2. Why has the poet used phrases like \u2018funny sounding sight\u2019 and \u2018funny feeling sound\u2019 with reference to the funny man?\nAnswer:\nThe poet uses these phrases to emphasise the man\u2019s absurd behaviour. \u201cFunny sounding sight\u201d suggests that his appearance is so strange it almost seems to make a sound, while \u201cfunny feeling sound\u201d indicates that his singing causes a physical sense of amusement. This blending of senses adds to the comic effect.\nVI. Can you think of any real-world situations where people do similar things for fun, entertainment, or performance? Share with your classmates and the teacher.\nAnswer:\nMany real-world examples resemble the funny man\u2019s playful behaviour:\nCircus clowns, who wear oversized costumes and perform silly acts.\nStreet performers who juggle strange objects or do unusual balancing tricks.\nComedians who use absurd props and illogical actions in their shows.\nChildren\u2019s entertainers who wear clothes backwards or put shoes on their hands to make kids laugh.\nPerformance artists who create surreal, nonsensical acts to surprise their audience.\nMime artists who pretend invisible objects are real in amusing ways.\nAll these examples involve breaking normal rules in a humorous, unexpected way to entertain people\u2014just like the funny man in the poem.\nClass 7 English A Funny Man Question Answer \u2013 Let us think and reflect (Pages 62-63)\nI. Read the extracts given below and answer the questions that follow:\nQuestion 1.He said, \u201cAllow me to present Your Highness with a rose. \u201d And taking out a currant bun He held it to my nose. I staggered back against the wall And then I answered, \u201cWell!\u201d\n(i) Why does the funny man address the poet as \u2018Your Highness\u2019?\nAnswer:\nHe calls her \u201cYour Highness\u201d to playfully imitate formal politeness in a humorous way.\n(ii) Choose a phrase from the extract which indicates a polite request.\nAnswer:\n\u201cAllow me to present\u201d expresses a courteous request.\n(iii) Choose the option which shows a \u2018currant bun\u2019.\nAnswer:\nImage 2 shows a currant bun.\n(Image 1 is bread, Image 3 is a donut, and Image 4 is a loaf.)\n(iv) Complete the sentence with an appropriate reason.\nWhen the poet says, \u2018Well!\u2019, it expresses surprise. This was so because _________________.\nAnswer:\nThe man gave her a currant bun instead of a rose, which was quite unexpected.\nQuestion 2. You never heard in all your life Such a funny feeling sound.\n\u201cMy friend, why do you wear two hats Upon your feet?\u201d I said.\nHe turned the other way about,\nAnd hopped home on his head.\n(i) Choose the line from the extract which tells us that the sound was unique.\nAnswer:\nYou had never heard a sound so strangely amusing in your entire life.\n(ii) Complete the sentence with an appropriate reason.\nThe poet calls the funny man \u2018my friend\u2019 because ___________.\nAnswer:\nThe poet refers to him as \u201cmy friend\u201d because she sees him as harmless and amusing.\n(iii) What does the reaction of the funny man to the poet\u2019s question tell us about him?\nAnswer:\nHis response shows he likes acting silly and doesn\u2019t treat things seriously.\n(iv) Choose the correct option to complete the sentence.\nThe last line of the extract makes the readers feel ___________.\nA. dreamy\nB. impatient\nC. worried\nD. cheerful\nAnswer:\nD. cheerful\nII. Answer the following questions:\nQuestion 1. Which character trait of the funny man was most appealing to you? Why?\nAnswer:\nHis playful nature is charming because it brings joy even without any logic.\nQuestion 2. The funny man does unusual things in the poem. How does it affect the overall mood of the poem?\nAnswer:\nThe funny man behaves in strange ways in the poem, and his actions add a cheerful, humorous tone throughout.\nQuestion 3. What alternative title would you suggest for the poem? Give reason(s) for your choice.\nAnswer:\nI would title it \u201cThe Topsy-Turvy Man\u201d because it perfectly reflects his upside-down behaviour.\nQuestion 4. Why do you think the poet has included dialogues in the poem?\nAnswer:\nThe poet uses dialogue in the poem because it makes the scene vivid and allows readers to feel the interaction directly.\nQuestion 5. What does the poet wish to convey by highlighting the unusual behaviour of the funny man?\nAnswer:\nThe poet emphasises the funny man\u2019s absurd actions to show that breaking rules can bring joy and surprise. His nonsensical behaviour celebrates imagination and the freedom to be delightfully unconventional.\nA Funny Man Class 7 Questions and Answers \u2013 Let us learn (Pages 63-65)\nRead the following phrases from the poem.\nwalking down\ntaking out\nsat down\nThese are phrasal verbs.\nPhrasal verbs are combinations of a verb with a preposition or adverb that together function as a single unit. They often have meanings that are different from the individual words used alone. The same verb followed by different prepositions or adverbs conveys different meanings.\nI. Match the phrasal verbs in Column 1 with their correct meaning in Column 2.\nAnswer:"
