      "q": "5. Toby: I can see beyond mountains and clouds, woods and even metals.\nNCERT Class 7 English Poorvi Chapter 7 Question Answer \u2013 Let us speak (Page 105)\nI. Read the following sentences from the text.\nAs usual, I had to be involved, though I\u2019d have preferred to finish my novel \u201cWe\u2019re old enough to look after ourselves, Ma, \u201d I said.\nThe word \u2018though\u2019 is pronounced to rhyme with the word \u2018low\u2019. The word \u2018enough\u2019 is pronounced to rhyme with the word \u2018puff.\nbough \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 dough\nthrough \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 cough\nthought \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 plough\nIn English there are many words ending with \u2013 ough, which are pronounced very differently. Listen to the teacher say these words aloud and notice how they are pronounced.\nNow work in pairs. Pronounce these words out loud and put them in the correct boxes given below.\nSounds like \u2018oo\u2019\nSounds like \u2018off\u2019\nSounds like \u2018o\u2019\nSounds like \u2018ow\u2019\nSounds like \u2018aw\u2019\nShare more words that sound similar to the words in the table.",
      "a": "wer:\nSounds like \u2018oo\u2019\nSounds like \u2018off\u2019\nSounds like \u2018o\u2019\nSounds like \u2018ou\u2019\nSounds like \u2018aw\u2019\nthrough\ncough\ndough\nplough\nought\ntrough\nthought\nbough\nnought\nbought\nII. Read the story given below aloud. Check the correct pronunciation with the teacher.\nIn spite of a nagging cough, the woman continued working in her farm, right through the rough weather. She filed up the trough with water for her cows and ploughed the fields to plant the seeds. She then plucked some raw mangoes from a low bough of a mango tree to make a spicy chutney. Though the day had been tough, she smiled peacefully as she had already prepared dough to make enough rotis for dinner.\nAnswer:\nStudents should do it by themselves.\nIII. Now try saying this tongue twister fast as many times as you can clearly.\nThe boy with the tough dough on the rose bough fell in the trough and caught a cough.\nAnswer:\nStudents should do it by themselves.\nClass 7th English Chapter 7 My Brother\u2019s Great Invention Question Answer \u2013 Let us write (Pages 106-107)\nThink about different vocations that you see around or you are currently studying.\nNow, choose a vocation and write a paragraph. You may use the hints given below.\nwhy you like this vocation\nwhat training is needed\nwhat tools and equipment are required\nwho would your co-workers be\nhighlight the importance of team work\nhow you would deal with the customers\nhow you plan to pursue it with a sense of pride\nAnswer:\nMy Dream Vocation \u2013 Becoming a Chef\nI want to become a chef when I grow up because I enjoy cooking and experimenting with new recipes. To achieve this, I will attend culinary school to learn cooking skills, kitchen safety, food presentation, and gain hands-on experience in a real kitchen. I will work alongside other chefs, kitchen staff, waiters, and restaurant managers as a team to ensure that customers have a wonderful dining experience. Teamwork is very important in a kitchen, and I will take pride in my work, stay creative, and aim for excellence while interacting with customers, listening to their feedback, and offering great service.\nMy Brother\u2019s Great Invention Class 7 NCERT Solutions \u2013 Let us explore (Pages 107-108)\nCreativity is \u2018thinking new things\u2019; innovation is \u2018doing new things\u2019 and an invention is \u2018bringing ideas or objects together in a novel way to create something that did not exist before\u2019. All it takes is a simple idea.\nHuman beings are naturally very curious and creative, and this has led to many scientific and technological inventions. You must have heard about many famous inventions. Some of them are mentioned below. You may look for the names of more such Indian inventors and their inventions.\nSushruta (sixth century B.C.), was a pioneer fiure in medical science. His innovations in surgery paved the way for modern surgery.\nDr. Janaki Ammal (Padma Shri) was a brilliant woman scientist of India who made a signifiant contribution to the field of cytogenetics\nUnder the directorship of Dr. A.P.J. Abdul Kalam (Bharat Ratna), Indian Space Research Organisation (ISRO) launched the satellite Rohini in near-earth orbit with India\u2019s fist Satellite Launch Vehicle in 1980 (the project was named \u2018SLV-III\u2019)\nDr. Narinder Singh Kapany (Padma Vibhushan) is known as Father of Fibre Optics. He played a pivotal role in inventing fire optics. He was named one of the seven \u2018Unsung Heroes of the Twentieth Century\u2019 for his invention.\nAnswer:\nStudents should do it by themselves.\nUnderstanding NCERT Solutions Class 7 English Chapter 7 My Brother\u2019s Great Invention\nMastering\nNCERT Solutions Class 7 English Chapter 7 My Brother\u2019s Great Invention\nhelps students develop creativity and analytical thinking. This chapter invites learners to reflect on inventions, family bonding, and innovative spirit\u2014skills crucial for academic success and personal growth.\nBy practicing the chapter exercises and reading the answers, students improve\ncomprehension and exam performance\n. Focus on the character traits and cause-and-effect events to increase your confidence while answering application-based questions in the 2026-27 session.\nRevise key events and traits highlighted in the story to strengthen your\nEnglish preparation\n. These NCERT solutions support concept clarity and make revision easier\u2014ensuring you\u2019re ready to excel in your exams!"
